{"name":"Solar Relic","hex":"#d2ab51","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"solar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the sun; proceeding from the sun.","Born under the predominant influence of the sun."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ellipsis of solar energy.","A fictional currency."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/solar"},{"term":"relic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["That which remains; that which is left after loss or decay; a remaining portion.","Something old and outdated, possibly kept for sentimental reasons."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause (an object) to appear old or worn, to distress."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/relic"}],"entries":[{"title":"Relic","blocks":[{"text":"In religion, a relic is an object or article of religious significance from the past. It usually consists of the physical remains or personal effects of a saint or other person preserved for the purpose of veneration as a tangible memorial. Relics are an important aspect of some forms of Buddhism, Hinduism, Christianity, Islam, Shamanism, and many other religions. Relic derives from the Latin reliquiae, meaning \"remains\", and a form of the Latin verb relinquere, to \"leave behind, or abandon\". A reliquary is a shrine that houses one or more religious relics."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Chef_de_Saint_Yves.jpg/330px-Chef_de_Saint_Yves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relic","wikidata":"Q187616"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Shrine_of_Saint_Lachtin%27s_Arm_NMI.jpg/500px-Shrine_of_Saint_Lachtin%27s_Arm_NMI.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Shrine_of_Saint_Lachtin%27s_Arm_NMI.jpg/1280px-Shrine_of_Saint_Lachti